 notes='Atkin page 6, with typed 5 crossed and handwritten 6 added, opens subsection 3.2. It records a 1962 result, a proof for alpha≤4 by hand computation, the subsequent general proof in 1963 prompted by a numerical error, and the Atlas computations after the author’s 1964 appointment. The footnote cites ATKIN and O’BRIEN(1967). The source’s word diisible lacks a separate v; this slip remains. The overwritten subsection digits and old digit in a reference are uncertain, while the live replacements are clear. Two references use comma separators, including the corrected reading of the subsequent proof reference; the still obtained (3,7) is already faithful in C. The sheet ends with b(7)= and a wavy continuation mark.\n\nS046-PROPAGATION: Assuming (3.7), if c(13^alpha n_0) is zero modulo 13^beta with beta≤alpha, each successive application at alpha, alpha+1, … preserves zero modulo 13^beta. For positive fixed n_0 the resulting indices are distinct, proving the stated infinitely-often consequence. S046-RANDOM-HEURISTIC: 40960/13^4=40960/28561≈1.434123455, so the displayed approximation 1.4 is numerically sound. Uniform independent residues would make this the expected count of zero residues modulo 13^4; the page expressly presents randomness as a supposition, not an established property. The reported largest observed b(n)=3 and full computational range 1≤n≤40960 are recorded as source testimony; those large computations are not repeated.',
